Good context to have — I checked the actual numbers instead of guessing, and they clarify what's happening.

Current state (confirmed in code): every turn action fires the exact same pulse — turn_speed=0.20 rad/s × turn_pulse_duration=0.35s ≈ 4° nominal per pulse — whether it's ALIGN_WITH_LANDMARK (meant to be a tiny nudge) or NAVIGATE_TO_FRONTIER/FOLLOW_DIRECTION (meant to explore in a new direction). Forward pulses are similarly fixed and small: 0.12 m/s × 0.40s ≈ 4.8cm. And the prompt in action_generator.py explicitly tells the model not to specify duration/velocity/step-count — it only ever picks direction + qualitative state (near/far, left/center/right), never a magnitude. There's also no odometry/IMU feedback anywhere in the repo — robot_safety.py itself flags that as future work — so every pulse is a blind, open-loop timed guess with no way to confirm how far the robot actually moved.

That combination — one-size-fits-all turn magnitude, tiny forward steps, and zero feedback — is a textbook setup for the left/right hunting you're describing: a fine correction and a "look somewhere completely different" turn get treated identically, so it easily overshoots small corrections and undershoots big reorientations.

Should the model output exact distance/degrees? No — keep that prohibition. VLMs are unreliable at estimating metric distance from a single monocular frame, and because this is open-loop, a bad number isn't caught until the next full image cycle. That's not a limitation specific to your setup; it's why most real VLM/LLM-navigation systems (VLN, LM-Nav, ViNT-style pipelines) keep the model's output categorical and push the actual metric conversion into code.

What real systems do instead, roughly in increasing effort:

1. Magnitude tiers, not raw numbers — let the model pick from a small enum (small/large turn, or reuse the existing alignment-vs-exploration action distinction that already exists in your code) and map each tier to its own calibrated duration. This is the cheapest fix and needs no schema change at all for turns — ALIGN_WITH_LANDMARK/cue_alignment vs. NAVIGATE_TO_FRONTIER/route_alignment/direction_turn are already distinct code paths sharing one constant; splitting that constant into a fine value and a coarse value alone should reduce hunting.
2. Grounded magnitude instead of guessed magnitude — if a landmark/doorway's position in the image is known, compute the actual heading correction from its pixel offset relative to the camera's horizontal FOV, rather than trusting the model's qualitative judgment at all. More accurate, moderate effort.
3. LiDAR-scaled forward distance — this directly answers your "door is 1m away, walk 1m not forever" question. Once the LiDAR gate exists, instead of a pure block/allow decision it can also report how much clear distance is ahead, and the forward pulse duration can be scaled to that (capped at a max), so the robot advances toward something close and stops appropriately instead of crawling in fixed 4.8cm increments. This is a natural extension of the LiDAR work already planned, not a separate project.
4. True closed-loop stopping (odometry/heading feedback ends the pulse when the target is reached, not a timer) — the real fix for open-loop drift, but needs sensor/SDK support that doesn't exist here yet and is more effort.

My recommendation: do #1 first — it's a same-day, zero-risk change (just recalibrating existing constants, no model/schema touch) and directly targets the imbalance you noticed. See if that alone kills the hunting before taking on #2 or #3. #3 is worth planning for once the LiDAR gate lands anyway, since you're about to have real range data in hand.

