Long-term pick between #1/#2/#3: #3 (LiDAR-grounded distance) and #2 (vision-grounded turning) are the real long-term architecture — they solve different axes of the same problem, not competing options. If I have to rank them:

- #3 first. It's grounded in a physical measurement (range), not a model's visual judgment, so it generalizes to any building without retuning — a 1m gap reads as 1m whether it's a hallway in this lab or a corridor somewhere else. It also reuses the sensor you're already installing for safety, so there's no separate investment. This is the one I'd treat as the backbone.
- #2 second, as the complement for turning: correcting heading toward a visible target from its pixel offset is pure image geometry (camera FOV math), so it's equally portable across buildings — but it only applies when something is actually visible to aim at.
- #1 stays as the fallback, not the end-state: for pure exploration turns where nothing is visible yet (frontier search, no landmark grounded), you still need some fixed step, so keep tiers there. But tiers alone don't generalize well — a "medium turn" calibrated in this lab's hallway width isn't a principled quantity anywhere else, it's just a tuned constant.

So: build #3 and #2 as the real system, keep #1 permanently as the no-target fallback rather than trying to make it the whole solution.

#4 — what's actually needed, and I found something concrete rather than guessing: temp_files/LiDAR_details.txt (already captured from the robot) shows a /high_state topic (unitree_legged_msgs/HighState, ~50Hz) with IMU orientation at /high_state/imu/rpy — roll/pitch/yaw already available, no new hardware or SDK needed. That file also explicitly notes no odometry topic is currently published.

That splits #4 into two very different-cost pieces:

1. Closed-loop turning (cheap, ready now) — yaw is already on the wire. A turn pulse could read current yaw before starting, compute target yaw = current + desired delta, and stop the twist publish when yaw crosses that target (with a timeout cap as a safety fallback, since IMU yaw can drift/jitter). No new sensor, no new SDK — just consuming a topic that's already flowing but currently unused. This is the highest-value/lowest-cost piece of #4 and would directly fix the turn-overshoot half of the hunting problem.
2. Closed-loop forward distance (harder) — no odometry topic exists, so "how far did I actually walk" isn't available for free. Options, increasing effort:
  - Check whether /high_state itself carries velocity or position-estimate fields beyond IMU (Unitree's HighState message sometimes includes forward/side speed or a fused position estimate even without a dedicated /odom topic) — worth a rosmsg show unitree_legged_msgs/HighState next time you're at the robot, since if it's already there this becomes as cheap as the yaw case.
  - If not, integrate commanded speed × time as a rough dead-reckoning estimate (still open-loop underneath, just a better guess than a fixed timer) — cheap but not truly closed-loop.
  - If real closed-loop forward stopping matters, LiDAR-based scan matching (comparing consecutive point clouds to estimate how far you moved) or visual odometry from the existing camera — both real engineering projects on top of the minimal LiDAR gate, not a quick add.

Given that, I'd sequence it: ship the LiDAR gate → add #3 (LiDAR-scaled forward pulses, solves distance without needing odometry) → add yaw-closed-loop turning (cheap, /high_state is already there) → only chase full forward odometry/SLAM if #3's range-capped approach turns out insufficient.
