First, a blocking unknown I can't resolve from code — which mode do you actually run?
thinkpad_robot_capture_to_amd.py supports --camera-mode {single, stitched, separate} (default single). If your real runs use stitched/separate (3 cameras), the scope below applies in full. If you actually run single (one camera), this gets much simpler — just one FOV to measure, no multi-camera mounting calibration. Which do you use day-to-day?

Assuming stitched/3-camera mode, here's what's missing and how to get each piece:

1. Per-camera horizontal FOV — not in any spec, code, or config; no camera model/vendor string exists anywhere in the repo to look one up from either. Must be measured directly:
  - Simplest method, no special tools: stand the robot a measured distance D from a flat wall, find the wall points where each camera's left and right frame edges fall (nudge a marker until it just leaves the frame), measure the physical distance W between those two marks, then FOV = 2 × atan((W/2) / D). Same spirit as the tape-measure LiDAR height check you already did.
  - Do this once for the front camera, then spot-check left/right — likely the same lens/FOV if all three are the same camera model, but that's an assumption worth verifying, not asserting.
2. Camera mounting bearing (left/right cameras' angular offset from true forward) — also nowhere in the repo. robot_executor.py already assumes "evidence_view==LEFT → turn left" but no actual degree value is coded anywhere. To measure: place a marker at a few known bearings around the robot (e.g. tape-measure/protractor marks on the floor at 0°/45°/90° from the robot's forward centerline) and note which camera panel each one lands in and roughly where — this tells you each camera's boresight angle relative to the robot's own heading, which you need to convert "offset within the LEFT panel" into a real-world turn angle rather than just an offset-from-that-camera's-own-center.
3. Panel geometry (already known, no measurement needed) — confirmed in code: stitched image is LEFT-FRONT-RIGHT, each panel is --stitch-width (default 960px) wide, no cropping/distortion between raw capture and panel by default since capture size and stitch size match. So pixel-to-panel mapping is already fixed and reliable; only the FOV and mounting-angle numbers are missing.
4. Schema field pairs with the existing evidence_view, not replacing it — the model already labels which panel (LEFT/FRONT/RIGHT) a landmark is in; #2 would add a numeric fraction (-1.0 to +1.0) for position within that panel. Worth knowing: evidence_view itself is model-inferred, not pixel-measured by the pipeline, so the new fraction's accuracy is bounded by how well the VLM judges its own image, on top of the geometry.
5. One caveat regardless of the above: even with a perfect target angle, converting it to a pulse duration still depends on turn_speed being an accurate real angular velocity — which is still open-loop and unvalidated (that's #4's territory, pending tomorrow's /high_state check). #2 gets you a better target angle; it doesn't by itself fix execution accuracy the way closed-loop yaw would.

