but 0.4s is tooo short for the navigation right? just asking

Good question, but I think it's a mix-up between two different things — 0.4s isn't how far the robot moves per navigation step, it's just how often it pauses to recheck during a longer move.

The actual per-action distance/angle isn't capped at 0.4s at all. A single NAVIGATE_TO_FRONTIER forward action can run up to ~5-7.5s (depending on forward_speed), covering up to 0.6m in one go. A single ALIGN_WITH_LANDMARK vision-grounded turn can run up to ~7.85s for a 90° turn. You already saw both of these for real: LiDAR_testing_1.txt had a forward pulse running 10×0.4s chunks (4.2s total) as one action, and LiDAR_testing_2.txt had the LEFT-camera turn running 20×0.4s chunks (7.85s total) as one action. So navigation-per-step is not short — it's actually much longer per step than it used to be, which was the whole point of #3.

0.4s is just the re-check interval inside that longer pulse — after every 0.4s, it briefly stops, checks (LiDAR clearance for forward, or just creates a pause point for turns), and continues if still clear. That's what lets a 5-second forward pulse actually stop 1.6 seconds in if something walks into the path, instead of committing blind for the full 5 seconds.

Worth knowing where 0.4s itself came from: it's not an arbitrary small number picked for this feature — it's literally the size every single pulse in this whole system used to be, for every action, before any of #1/#2/#3 existed (the original forward_pulse_duration/turn_pulse_duration defaults were 0.40s/0.35s). So chunking didn't shrink anything; it reused the system's original per-action granularity as the internal safety heartbeat inside actions that are now deliberately longer than that.

The real trade-off, which I did flag earlier: the robot fully stops and restarts every 0.4s during a long pulse rather than gliding continuously — so it'll look like a rapid series of small halting steps rather than one smooth motion. That's a real cost (some lost time/smoothness at each boundary), traded for the safety property above. If that stop-start pattern turns out to feel too choppy in practice, the chunk size (MOTION_WATCH_CHUNK_SECONDS) is the knob to loosen — but I'd want to see it run first before touching it.
