So if there is a obstacle, the robot passes stop command as of now, then will it continue exploring or just stops. I am thinking of doing a re-direction, what do you think? give me suggestions

Good question — let me trace exactly what happens, since it's more than just "the robot stops."

Current behavior, confirmed in code:

1. The blocked pulse aborts (safety_blocked, executed=False) — the robot itself just doesn't move for that one action.
2. The outer loop does not stop the run. It reports the failure back to the AMD server via /autonomous/ack (since your setup uses --execute confirm + --mode auto --memory true, this path is active), which appends the failure reason to NavigationMemory.failed_actions.
3. The last 3 entries of failed_actions get injected into the next planning prompt via context_for_planner() — so the model literally sees something like "ThinkPad did not execute NAVIGATE_TO_FRONTIER: Motion blocked: nearest obstacle at 0.45m, below the 0.50m stop distance." before deciding the next action.

So it's not blind repetition by design — the model gets a hint that the last attempt failed and why. But there's no guarantee it does anything smart with that hint: since the robot didn't move, the next image looks the same, and the model has to infer "try a different direction" purely from the failure text plus whatever it can see in that same image. It could just as easily retry the identical action, or get stuck oscillating.

My suggestion — check before building anything. This feedback path is already live in your setup at zero extra cost. Next time you hit a safety_blocked in a real run, look specifically at what the model does on the following step — does it pick something else (turn, search, alternate landmark), or does it repeat the same blocked action? That single data point tells you whether this is actually a problem worth solving, versus something the existing text hint already handles reasonably.

If it turns out the model does get stuck, here's how I'd escalate, roughly by effort:

1. Richer block signal (moderate) — right now the LiDAR bridge only reports the forward ±20° cone. It's already scanning 360°; cheaply add a couple more azimuth-filtered queries (e.g. a left-ish and right-ish cone) and fold "left clearer (Xm) vs right clearer (Ym)" into the block reason text. This gives the model an actual data-grounded hint instead of asking it to guess blind from a static image — keeps the architecture's existing philosophy intact (VLM still decides, just with better information).
2. Auto-redirect after repeated blocks (larger) — track consecutive safety_blocked results for the same action type; after some threshold, have the executor itself do a small reactive turn toward whichever direction the LiDAR shows clearer, then hand back to the VLM to re-plan from the new heading. This is a real behavior change (the executor making a decision, not just executing one) and a bigger step — I'd only reach for it if #1 doesn't fix it.

I'd start with checking real behavior first, then #1 before #2 — don't build the bigger mechanism before confirming the cheap one doesn't already cover it.
