Is it possible to give two commands like turn right and move forward in a single step?

Technically possible to build, but I'd recommend against it — here's the tradeoff.

The whole pipeline is built around re-observing after every motion type change (capture → plan → pulse → recapture), specifically because turn precision isn't guaranteed — you've already seen this directly: the LEFT-boresight turn in the C2.016 run wasn't perfectly on-target, and the pipeline only recovered because it looked again before committing to the next move (re-found the target as a new landmark L008 instead of blindly walking forward on a bad heading). If turn+forward were chained into one step with no recapture between them, a turn that over/undershoots would send the forward pulse in a subtly wrong direction with zero chance to catch it until after both have already executed — the exact failure mode the current step-by-step structure exists to avoid. That's also why ALIGN_WITH_LANDMARK and APPROACH_LANDMARK are two separate skills/steps rather than one, even though they often happen back-to-back.

Worth noting: the main reason you'd want this — fewer round-trips, more progress per step — is already largely solved by #1/#2/#3. A single turn or forward action now covers a lot more (up to ~90° or ~0.6m) than the old fixed tiny pulses did, so the number of steps needed for a given maneuver has already dropped a lot without needing to bundle different motion types together.

If there's a specific scenario driving this (e.g. "there's a gap 2m ahead and slightly right, I want one maneuver to get there") — that might be better served by a genuinely new single primitive (a diagonal/curved approach) rather than chaining turn+forward, which keeps the re-observe-between-motions safety property intact. Happy to think through that if that's the actual case you're hitting.
