1. Did the model fail to read the sign?

I pulled up the actual image (robot_live_frames/step_0002/front.png) and yes — there's real evidence of a misread. The sign clearly shows two rows: a left arrow for rooms C0.016–C0.020, and a red right arrow for rooms C0.004–C0.008 (your target). The correct move from that sign was right.

What actually happened: step 2's action (ALIGN_WITH_LANDMARK toward the sign itself, turning left) isn't the problem — that's just centering on the sign object to look at it, not following its arrow yet, and is legitimate. The real issue is what happened after: steps 3 and 4 picked entirely different landmarks (open doorways further left) instead of coming back to read and act on the sign's right-arrow, and the robot kept drifting left/forward for several more steps. It only found room C0.004 later via a second, different sign around step 10-11.

Likely reasons, roughly in order of how much I trust them:
- Fine-grained sign parsing is the weak point, not general OCR. This sign has two stacked rows, each with an icon cluster, an arrow, and a room range — correctly pairing "this arrow belongs to this specific range" is a harder spatial-reasoning task than just detecting "there's a sign here." The model's own landmark description ("listing room ranges including C0.004") shows it noticed the sign had relevant text, but never captured which arrow went with C0.004 specifically.
- The model didn't follow its own prompted fallback rule. action_generator.py's prompt explicitly says: if signage is unclear, move closer / don't guess, and say so in the reason. Here it didn't do either — it just moved on to a different, easier-to-grab landmark (a doorway) instead of re-engaging with the sign.
- Possible model capacity limit. If the deployed model is a quantized local model (I recall a README_GGUF_Q8.md reference from earlier work in this codebase), that's consistent with weaker fine-grained multi-element sign parsing than a full-precision or larger frontier model would manage.
- Image quality itself is not the likely cause — I looked at the actual 960×720 capture and the sign is clearly legible even to my eyes; this doesn't look like a resolution/blur problem.

2. Why the LiDAR scan goes stale — found the actual cause

This is a real, fixable bug, not sensor flakiness, and "front was clear" is exactly what I'd expect given the cause. The "stale scan" message isn't a false obstacle detection — it's the safety gate refusing to trust old data, unrelated to whether anything's actually in front.

Root cause, confirmed in code: a brand-new RobotSafetyMonitor (and therefore a brand-new PersistentLidarBridge subprocess) gets spawned from scratch on every single step (thinkpad_robot_capture_to_amd.py:1163, inside maybe_execute_robot_action, called once per auto-step). Unlike the cmd_vel bridge — which is deliberately cached and reused across the whole run via _get_shared_bridge/_SHARED_BRIDGES specifically to avoid paying ROS registration cost repeatedly — the LiDAR bridge has no equivalent caching. So every step pays full ROS node registration + resubscription cost again, and nothing ever shuts the previous ones down (only atexit-registered, which only fires once at final process exit) — so by step 9+ there could be 8+ orphaned LiDAR-bridge subprocesses still alive and registered with the ROS master, competing for the same topic subscription.

This matches the actual pattern exactly: steps 1-8 all succeeded cleanly (little/no pileup yet), then staleness started appearing from step 9 onward (9, 13, 14) — right when accumulated subprocess/registration load would start mattering. I confirmed via the saved JSONs that "sensor_ready": true in all three stale cases — the bridge had connected and gotten at least one scan, it just didn't get a fresh one in time for the query, consistent with registration-latency/contention rather than a dead sensor.

Fix (not applied yet, since you didn't ask for a code change): apply the same shared/cached bridge pattern already used for PersistentCmdVelBridge to the LiDAR safety monitor, so one bridge subprocess is reused for the whole run instead of respawned every step.

3. Would a RealSense D435/D435i help?

Not for this specific problem. The D435's RGB stream isn't meaningfully sharper than your current C920 for OCR/sign-reading purposes — the image I checked was already clear, so the bottleneck is the model's fine-grained reading/reasoning (per Q1), not camera optics. Swapping cameras wouldn't fix that.

Where a RealSense would genuinely add value is its actual differentiator — depth sensing (and IMU on the "i" variant) — which could complement or extend obstacle detection beyond the LiDAR, or potentially support visual-inertial odometry later. But that's a different project (new capture pipeline, drivers, and redoing the FOV/boresight calibration work from this session) aimed at a different problem than sign-reading. I wouldn't reach for it to fix issue #1.

