1. Repeated blocked actions → should we do #1?

I traced how the pipeline currently reacts to a safety_blocked result, and found something that changes the recommendation:

What actually happens today, confirmed in code:
- When an action fails to execute (executed=False, e.g. LiDAR-blocked), the ThinkPad script's two repeat-detection guards (apply_no_progress_guard, apply_action_retry_guard in thinkpad_robot_capture_to_amd.py) both reset their tracking state to None on that exact step (line 1646-1651: elif ...: last_executed_state = None). Neither guard accumulates across consecutive blocks — they only guard against a successfully executed action producing an unchanging result. So there's currently no mechanism at all that detects "N consecutive obstacle blocks" and intervenes — I'd assumed earlier that the pulse-count retry guard could be reused for this; it can't, it doesn't apply to this failure mode.
- Meanwhile, the LiDAR-block reason text does reach the planner: on the ack round-trip, NAV.acknowledge_execution() calls memory.add_failed_action(reason) (navigator.py:412), and failed_actions (last 3 entries) is included in the JSON blob sent to the model every step (memory.py:865-868).
- But — the action-generator prompt never explains what failed_actions means or tells the model to do anything differently when it sees one. It's raw data in the context with zero instruction attached, same class of gap as the extra.direction field we just fixed.

So the mechanism producing what you're seeing is simplest read as: robot blocked → doesn't move → next frame looks the same → model has no instruction to treat "recently blocked" as a signal to change plan → proposes something similar again → repeats → you intervene manually.

Revised recommendation: #1 (richer left/right LiDAR cone data) is good, but data alone won't fix this — it needs a paired prompt rule telling the model to use failed_actions and change direction/action when it sees a recent obstacle block. That's still moderate effort, just two changes instead of one. Given this model's track record of not reliably following soft fallback instructions (same pattern as the sign-arrow miss), I'd treat that combo as "try first," but go in expecting it might not be fully reliable, since it still depends on the model choosing to act on the hint. #2 (executor-driven auto-redirect) would need genuinely new counting logic now — not the free reuse I originally implied — but it's the one that doesn't depend on model compliance at all, which matters given the fix here is specifically about getting the robot unstuck without you.

