This one's worth understanding precisely before you touch anything: forward_speed is used two ways — as the literal linear_x published to /cmd_vel, and as the divisor in the LiDAR-scaled pulse-duration formula (duration = safe_distance / forward_speed). Because it's the same constant in both places, if the real-vs-commanded relationship is linear (which 3 tight samples at one speed can't fully confirm, but is consistent with), changing the --forward-speed CLI value won't fix the ~25% distance overshoot — the error cancels back in algebraically regardless of what value you pick, since duration and real speed scale together. Worked example: for a 0.6m safe-distance pulse, commanded duration = 2.0s, but real distance covered = 0.375 × 2.0 = 0.75m — a real 0.15m overshoot past the intended stop margin.

The mitigating factor: motion-watch chunking (0.4s rechecks) already bounds most of this — each chunk only accumulates ≈3cm of extra blind travel (0.15m vs. 0.12m assumed) before the next LiDAR recheck, not the full 0.15m in one blind shot. So the real exposure is small, not the worst-case number above. A proper fix would be a separate distance-math correction factor (e.g. divide the duration formula by the measured ~1.25 ratio) distinct from the /cmd_vel speed constant — that's a real code change, not something to do by editing a CLI flag, and I'd want more than 3 samples at one speed before trusting a correction factor. Not urgent for today given chunking's mitigation; worth a proper pass later with more data.

