Implemented. Here's what it does and the key design decisions:

What it does

New opt-in flag --auto-redirect-after-blocks N (default 0 = disabled, matching how --use-yaw-feedback was introduced as opt-in until proven on hardware). When N consecutive obstacle blocks happen, the executor turns toward whichever side LiDAR reports clearer — without waiting for the VLM to notice and self-correct.

Design decisions worth knowing

1. Counts consecutive blocks regardless of which landmark/action the model used to justify them. The original framing was "same action type," but today's run-1 evidence showed the model cycling through different landmark IDs (L029, L031, L033, L035...) while getting blocked at the exact same physical spot each time — so matching on action identity would never have triggered in the scenario that motivated this. I deliberately track physical-block-repetition, not decision-identity-repetition.
2. Scoped to route_advance/direction_advance motion kinds only — reused the existing LIDAR_SCALABLE_MOTION_KINDS constant from robot_executor.py rather than inventing a new one. This deliberately excludes cue_approach (CHECK_DOOR_LABEL/READ_SIGN/APPROACH_LANDMARK) and doorway_traversal (PASS_THROUGH_DOORWAY) — those legitimately hit the stop-distance boundary as part of "get as close as safely possible to read/pass through," and an auto-redirect turning the robot away mid-approach would actively undermine that, not help it.
3. Direction choice (_pick_redirect_direction): picks the larger of left_range_m/right_range_m; if one side is None (no point detected in that cone — genuinely open, not "unknown"), that side is preferred over a measured one, not avoided.
4. Reuses the existing FOLLOW_DIRECTION execution path — same maybe_execute_robot_action → SafeCmdVelExecutor → RobotSafetyMonitor machinery as every normal action, including the --execute confirm y/N prompt if that's how you're running. No new unsupervised motion path; the reason string printed ("Auto-redirect after N consecutive obstacle blocks...") makes it obvious in the console what's happening and why.
5. Doesn't consume a step from --max-steps — it runs within the current step's iteration, not as its own loop iteration, so it costs wall-clock time but not budget.
6. Counter resets to 0 after firing (whether or not a usable direction was found), so it can trigger again later in the run rather than only once.

